  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; Meowdoku Guide</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
  <SideAd />
    <h1>Meowdoku Guide: Hints, Strategy &amp; Games Like Meowdoku</h1>

    <p class="lede">Played LinkedIn Queens? Then you already know how to think in Meowdoku — same deduction, but every token is a cat. Below: the rules, six techniques that finish any board without the hint button, and fully verified answer boards for the early levels.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Oakever.</div>

    <h2>What Is Meowdoku?</h2>
    <p>Meowdoku is a free cat-themed placement puzzle by Oakever for iOS and Android. Settle one cat in each colored region — never two in the same row or column, never touching, corners included. Boards grow from 4×4 upward, and regions get more intricate as levels climb. Its closest cousin is <b>LinkedIn Queens</b>, not Sudoku.</p>

    <h2>Meowdoku Rules</h2>
    <div class="rulewrap">
      <div class="xdemo" id="xdemo"></div>
      <ol>
        <li><b>One cat per colored region.</b> Every color territory contains exactly one cat — no region left empty.</li>
        <li><b>One cat per row and column.</b> Cats never share a row or column, like rooks that can't see each other.</li>
        <li><b>Cats can't touch — even diagonally.</b> The moment a cat settles, the 8 cells around it are off-limits.</li>
        <li><b>Every board has exactly one solution.</b> It's always reachable by logic alone — if you're guessing, an earlier placement is wrong.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <h2>How to Play: Controls</h2>
    <p>Three clicks per cell: the <b>1st</b> click places an X mark (excluded cell), the <b>2nd</b> click places a cat, and the <b>3rd</b> click clears the cell. X marks are the pencil marks of Queens-style puzzles — use them liberally.</p>

    <div class="playwrap" id="playwrap">
      <MeowdokuPlay mode="random" />
    </div>

    <h2>6 Solving Strategies</h2>
    <div class="strat">
      <div class="st"><i>1</i><span><b>Place single-cell regions first.</b> A region with one open cell settles itself.</span></div>
      <div class="st"><i>2</i><span><b>Follow row &amp; column pressure.</b> A row or column with one legal cell left is a locked placement.</span></div>
      <div class="st"><i>3</i><span><b>Draw the exclusion circle.</b> Before placing, mark the 8 cells a cat would block.</span></div>
      <div class="st"><i>4</i><span><b>Eliminate with X-marks.</b> Rule out candidates systematically — the last cat reveals itself.</span></div>
      <div class="st"><i>5</i><span><b>Prefer corners and edges.</b> Edge cells touch fewer neighbors, so they're safer.</span></div>
      <div class="st"><i>6</i><span><b>Re-scan after every cat.</b> Each placement cascades — always recheck the whole board.</span></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Meowdoku Level Answers: Level 1–3, Verified</h2>
    <div class="callout warn">⚠️ <span>Meowdoku can randomize boards based on player progress — the same level number may look different on your device. If your grid doesn't match the diagrams below, treat this walkthrough as a <b>derivation demo</b>, not coordinates to copy.</span></div>
    <div class="callout grn">✔ <span><b>Programmatically verified unique solutions</b>: all legal placements were enumerated for each board (one cat per row, column, and region; no two cats touching, diagonals included) — each level has exactly one solution, matching the official answers. Answer cells are rendered with the official cat sprite.</span></div>

    <!-- Level 1 -->
    <div class="boardrow">
      <div class="stepstrip">
        <div class="step"><div class="bd s4" id="l1s0"></div><i>Start</i></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="bd s4" id="l1s1"></div><i>S1 R1C3</i></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="bd s4" id="l1s2"></div><i>S2 R2C1</i></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="bd s4" id="l1s3"></div><i>S3 R3C4</i></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="bd s4" id="l1s4"></div><i>Done ✔</i></div>
      </div>
      <div class="lvmeta">
        <h4>Level 1 <span class="lvtag e">4×4 · Easy</span></h4>
        <ol>
          <li><b>R1C3</b> — the light-rose region has exactly one cell, so it settles itself (Strategy 1).</li>
          <li><b>R2C1</b> — same story: the deep-rose region is a single cell (Strategy 1).</li>
          <li><b>R3C4</b> — row 3's only legal cell: R3C3 sits under the column-3 cat, and R3C1 / R3C2 share or diagonally touch the R2C1 cat (Strategies 2 + 4).</li>
          <li><b>R4C2</b> — row 4 × column 2 is the only cell left (Strategy 2).</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="ans">Answer: R1C3 · R2C1 · R3C4 · R4C2</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Level 2 -->
    <div class="boardrow">
      <div class="bd b4" id="l2"></div>
      <div class="lvmeta">
        <h4>Level 2 <span class="lvtag e">4×4 · Easy</span></h4>
        <ol>
          <li><b>R1C2</b> — the light-rose region has a single cell (Strategy 1).</li>
          <li><b>R2C4</b> — every other row-2 cell touches or shares with the R1C2 cat, making this the row's only legal cell; it also covers the deep-rose region (Strategy 2).</li>
          <li><b>R3C1</b> — the purple region's only cell left standing in row 3 (Strategies 2 + 4).</li>
          <li><b>R4C3</b> — row 4 closes with its single legal cell (Strategy 2).</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="ans">Answer: R1C2 · R2C4 · R3C1 · R4C3</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Level 3 -->
    <div class="boardrow">
      <div class="bd b5" id="l3"></div>
      <div class="lvmeta">
        <h4>Level 3 <span class="lvtag m">5×5 · 5 cats</span></h4>
        <ol>
          <li><b>R1C3</b> / <b>R2C1</b> — two single-cell regions (light-rose and deep-rose), place both immediately (Strategy 1).</li>
          <li><b>R4C2</b> — the pink region has one cell (Strategy 1).</li>
          <li><b>R3C5</b> — every other purple cell is blocked by a row, column, or contact rule — the only legal home left (Strategies 2 + 4).</li>
          <li><b>R5C4</b> — the orange region × row 5 closes the board: every other orange cell touches or shares with an earlier cat (Strategy 2).</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="ans">Answer: R1C3 · R2C1 · R3C5 · R4C2 · R5C4</div>
      </div>
    </div>

