        <tr><th>Video walkthroughs</th><td>A real move order that is easy to follow along.</td><td>Slow to scrub, impossible to search, and one video covers exactly one board.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Auto-generated guide sites</th><td>A page per level number.</td><td>Sudoku-flavored tips that don't apply to this game, placeholder boards, and answers nobody verified.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="cmp-note">Every board answer on this page was verified by proving exactly one solution exists — if a layout can't be proven unique, we don't publish it as an answer.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details><summary>What is Meowdoku?</summary><p>Meowdoku is a free cat-themed placement puzzle by Oakever for iOS and Android. You settle exactly one cat in each colored region of a grid, under three constraints: one cat per row, one per column, and no two cats touching — corners and diagonals included. Boards grow from 4×4 upward and regions get more intricate as levels climb. Its closest cousin is LinkedIn Queens, not Sudoku.</p></details>

    <details><summary>What are the best Meowdoku hints for stuck players?</summary><p>Six no-hint techniques that solve any board: place single-cell regions first; follow row and column pressure; draw the exclusion circle of the 8 cells around every new cat; eliminate candidates systematically with X-marks; prefer corners and edges because they touch fewer neighbors; and re-scan the whole board after every placement. Together these make the hint button unnecessary.</p></details>

    <details><summary>Where can I find Meowdoku answers?</summary><p>Right here for Levels 1–3: each answer is shown on a real board with the reasoning behind every placement. One caveat — Meowdoku can randomize boards based on player progress, so the same level number may look different on your device. If your grid doesn't match, use the walkthrough as a derivation demo; the technique transfers to any board.</p></details>

    <details><summary>Is Meowdoku the same as regular Sudoku?</summary><p>No — and it is not a numbers game at all. Meowdoku is a Queens-style placement puzzle: one cat per colored region, one per row and column, with no two cats touching even diagonally. It shares only a spirit of pure-logic deduction with Sudoku. Its closest cousins are Star Battle and LinkedIn Queens.</p></details>

    <details><summary>What games are like Meowdoku?</summary><p>GridPaw's placement-logic lineup is the closest free browser match: Shikaku (partition the grid into numbered rectangles), Akari (place lights so every cell glows), Nurikabe (islands in a sea of black cells), Nonogram (picture logic from row and column clues), and Kakuro (cross sums). All are free in the browser with no download, and all share the calm single-solution feel.</p></details>

    <details><summary>Is Meowdoku good for your brain?</summary><p>Yes. Meowdoku's deduction core exercises logical reasoning, working memory, and concentration — the same mental muscles behind well-studied puzzle training. Regular play is linked in research to sharper performance on reasoning and memory tasks, and the cozy cat presentation makes the habit easy to keep.</p></details>

